<resources>
    <color name="purple_200">#FFBB86FC</color>
    <color name="purple_500">#FF6200EE</color>
    <color name="purple_700">#FF3700B3</color>
    <color name="teal_200">#FF03DAC5</color>
    <color name="teal_700">#FF018786</color>
    <color name="black">#FF000000</color>
    <color name="white">#FFFFFFFF</color>

    <!--
        The screen ground, mirroring the Bone token in ui/theme/Color.kt (contract C1).

        It exists as a resource for exactly one reason: Theme.SlowLock paints it as the window
        background, so a cold launch lands on the final colour instead of flashing the platform's
        white starting window and flipping to bone a frame later (contract C5, research R5). This
        is the same class of defect FR-030 guards on the wait screen; nobody had checked the main
        window for it.

        Duplicating a token across Kotlin and XML is a cost, not a pattern. It is paid here
        because a window background is resolved by the framework before any Compose code runs, so
        the Kotlin value is not reachable at the moment it is needed.
    -->
    <color name="screen_ground">#FFF3F0EA</color>

    <!--
        The wait screen's three values (feature 004, FR-027).

        Both are painted twice: by Theme.SlowLock.Wait's windowBackground, so the system's
        starting window is already the final colour on the tap's own frame, and by WaitScreen
        itself. Matching them is what removes the flash FR-030 forbids.

        WHAT CHANGED IN FEATURE 004, and why the old comment here is gone. Feature 003 justified
        this screen as "flat, unbranded and unsaturated", and said anything with character here
        was a defect. That was one means to an end, not the end. The binding property was always
        that the screen is STATIC and not worth reading twice - and a screen can be quiet while
        still belonging to the app it came from. These values are now the app's own ground, its
        muted ink and its accent (FR-037 amends 003's FR-022/FR-023 and the wait-screen
        contract's W12).

        What did NOT change: nothing here animates, nothing varies between apps, nothing counts
        down. The rule is unchanged; only the palette is.

        These deliberately mirror the Bone / Ink40 / Amber tokens in ui/theme/Color.kt rather
        than referencing them - WaitScreen resolves its own colours precisely so a change to the
        app's theme cannot reach it (FR-033). A values-night variant of all three is mandatory,
        not optional - see values-night/colors.xml.
    -->
    <color name="wait_background">#FFF3F0EA</color>
    <color name="wait_text">#FF6F6A5E</color>
    <color name="wait_rule">#FFC9821F</color>
</resources>